    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d578">

                <pb facs="0683" n="587" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg587"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Calvo.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-10-20"><hi rendition="#italic">October 20,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 23.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that,
                    satisfactory official assurance having been given that in the Republic of Costa
                    Rica the law permits to citizens of the United States the benefit of copyright
                    on substantially the same basis as to the citizens of that Republic, the
                    President has, by his proclamation of the 19th instant, declared and proclaimed
                    that the first of the conditions prescribed by section 13 of <pb facs="0684"
                        n="588" xml:id="pg588"/> the act approved March 3, 1891, now exists and is
                    fulfilled in respect to the citizens of the Republic of Costa Rica.</p>
                <p>I beg to inclose for your use printed copies of the proclamation<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d578-f1"/> referred to.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
